e are jumping back to the Creational category. You already mastered the standard Factory Method, which creates one
specific object.
The Abstract Factory is the "Factory of Factories." It solves a massive enterprise problem: enforcing that a group of
objects perfectly matches each other.

The Problem: The Incompatible Family Disaster
Imagine your company is building a multi-cloud application. You need to deploy a Server and a Storage database.
You support two providers: AWS (EC2 Server + S3 Storage) and Azure (VM Server + Blob Storage).

The Beginner Way:
The Junior Developer creates objects using if statements scattered all over the codebase:

Java
Server myServer;
Storage myStorage;

if (cloudProvider.equals("AWS")) {
    myServer = new AwsEc2Server();
    myStorage = new AwsS3Storage();
} else if (cloudProvider.equals("AZURE")) {
    myServer = new AzureVmServer();
    myStorage = new AzureBlobStorage(); // Oh wait, typo! The junior wrote AwsS3Storage here!
}

Why a Senior rejects this:

   1. The Mix-and-Match Bug: A developer could accidentally create an Azure Server and try to connect it to AWS Storage.
   The internal networks are incompatible, and the app crashes in production.
   2. Maintenance Nightmare: If you add Google Cloud (GCP) tomorrow, you have to hunt down every single if statement in
   50 different files to add the GCP objects.

The Senior Solution: The Abstract Factory
The Abstract Factory says: "Never let the client pick and choose individual parts. Force them to use a Factory that
produces guaranteed, matching families of objects."

We create a CloudFactory interface. The client just asks the factory for a "Server" and a "Storage". The client has no
idea if they are on AWS or Azure. The specific factory (like AwsFactory) guarantees that every object it spits out
belongs to the AWS family.